---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="2.1 Introduction to Human Resource Management">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/ibbm/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <a href="/ibbm/unit2/">Unit 2</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <span>2.1 Introduction to HRM</span>
  </div>
  <div class="topic-hero">
    <span class="topic-number">2.1</span>
    <h1>Introduction to Human Resource Management</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">What shapes HR decisions, and why people resist change.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabs">
    <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="switchTab(this,'notes')">Notes</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'practice')">Practice</button>
  </div>

  <!-- NOTES -->
  <div id="notes" class="tab-content active">

    <div class="learning-goals">
      <div class="learning-goals-title">Learning Goals</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Explain the role of HR management</li>
        <li>Explain internal and external factors that shape HR planning</li>
        <li>Explain why people resist change at work</li>
        <li>Evaluate HR strategies for reducing resistance to change</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">What HR does</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Workforce (HR) planning</strong></td><td>Working out how many people, and which roles, the business needs</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Recruitment</strong></td><td>Finding and hiring the right people</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Training</strong></td><td>Helping staff learn what they need to do the job well</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Appraisal</strong></td><td>Reviewing how staff are performing</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Termination or dismissal</strong></td><td>Managing people leaving, by choice or not</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Factors that shape HR planning</h2>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="content-card">
          <div class="content-card-title">Internal</div>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Size</strong> of the business</li>
            <li><strong>Budget</strong> for staffing</li>
            <li><strong>Promotion</strong>: who moves up, and the gaps they leave</li>
            <li><strong>Flexitime</strong>: staff choosing their hours within limits</li>
            <li><strong>Motivation</strong> and morale</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="content-card">
          <div class="content-card-title">External</div>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Sociocultural</strong>: demographic change, changing habits, attitudes to work</li>
            <li><strong>Technological</strong>: automation can replace or redesign roles</li>
            <li><strong>Economic</strong>: unemployment, labour mobility, immigration, the gig economy</li>
            <li><strong>Legal/political</strong>: minimum wage, employment law</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div>
        <p>Demographic change, labour mobility, immigration and the gig economy were added from the syllabus to fill a gap in the lesson slides.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Why people resist change</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Fear</strong> from misinformation or misunderstanding</li>
        <li><strong>Uncertainty</strong> over timing, impact, and who's affected</li>
        <li><strong>Self-interest</strong>: "my job matters, why change it?"</li>
        <li><strong>Low tolerance</strong>: "we're doing fine as it is"</li>
        <li><strong>Blame-shifting</strong>: "the problem is other departments"</li>
        <li>Workers fear for habits and job security; managers fear backlash and conflict</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="callout key-term">
        <div class="callout-title">The human side</div>
        <p>Textbooks rarely sound sympathetic to employees, so here's the balance: people have put a lot of blood, sweat and tears into their workplace. Change can feel personal, like throwing out work that's already been done. And staff often feel, or know, that management is out of touch.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Reducing resistance to change</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>How</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Participation</strong></td><td>Involve staff in discussing and deciding the change</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Planning</strong></td><td>Roll it out in stages, with feedback at each step</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Communication</strong></td><td>Keep everyone informed, bad news as well as good</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Negotiation</strong></td><td>Offer incentives that make the change easier to accept</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Manipulation</strong></td><td>Win over key staff representatives so others follow</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Coercion</strong></td><td>Force it through with threats; a last resort</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="callout key-term"><div class="callout-title">Upside</div><p>Builds trust and cuts resistance over time. Staged planning lets managers adjust. Incentives line up staff interests with the change.</p></div>
        <div class="callout warning"><div class="callout-title">Catch</div><p>Participation and planning slow down urgent change. Resistance often stays despite communication. Manipulation and coercion damage trust and morale.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-title">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>HR plans, recruits, trains, appraises and manages people leaving</li>
        <li>HR planning is shaped by internal factors (size, budget, promotion, flexitime, motivation) and external ones (STEEPLE)</li>
        <li>Resistance comes from fear, uncertainty, self-interest and low tolerance, and change can feel personal</li>
        <li>Participation, planning and communication build trust but take time; coercion is fast but costly</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div><!-- /notes -->

  <!-- PRACTICE -->
  <div id="practice" class="tab-content">

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 1: Brain dump</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Two minutes, no notes: list every internal and external factor affecting HR planning you can remember. Then check against the Notes tab.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 2: Become the expert</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Choose one factor (size, budget, promotion, flexitime, motivation, sociocultural, technological, economic, legal/political). Write:</div>
        <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>A definition</li>
          <li>How it affects HR planning, and which part of it</li>
          <li>Why it's not a trivial issue for an HR team</li>
          <li>One real-world example</li>
          <li>One exam-style question about it, and a model answer</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 3: SwiftHaul, find the evidence</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">A logistics company called SwiftHaul is deciding whether to hire an additional warehouse supervisor. Internally, the company's flat structure means one existing supervisor is already stretched across three shifts, the department's budget has just been increased after a strong quarter, and the business is small enough that one bad hire would be felt company-wide. A recent internal promotion has left a gap in the team, and management knows that staff morale and motivation dip whenever shifts are understaffed. Externally, low unemployment in the region is making it harder to find qualified candidates, rising minimum wage legislation means any new hire will cost more than the last one, growing demand for next-day delivery is increasing workload pressure, and warehouse automation raises the question of whether the role should be filled by a person at all.</p>
        <div class="exercise-q">Find the evidence for each factor.</div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Check</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">
            Size: small enough that one bad hire is felt company-wide.<br />
            Budget: just increased after a strong quarter.<br />
            Promotion: an internal promotion left a gap.<br />
            Motivation: morale dips when shifts are understaffed.<br />
            Economic: low unemployment, so candidates are hard to find.<br />
            Legal/political: rising minimum wage.<br />
            Sociocultural: demand for next-day delivery.<br />
            Technological: warehouse automation.
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 4: Uber, employee or contractor?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Use the factors to explain why Uber wants its drivers classified as contractors, and why courts in different countries haven't agreed.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 5: Same change, two announcements</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="two-col">
          <div class="content-card">
            <div class="content-card-title">Version A</div>
            <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
              <li>School locked down: 7am to 5pm</li>
              <li>No more learning at home. All work must be done during school hours</li>
              <li>All learning on paper from static textbooks. No laptops or iPads</li>
              <li>Lunches charged to school accounts: compulsory. Set lunches, no options</li>
              <li>Assessment every single class</li>
              <li>No more real assessments (except unavoidable IB exams)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="content-card">
            <div class="content-card-title">Version B</div>
            <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
              <li>More learning time at school: 7am to 5pm</li>
              <li>No homework. All work gets finished during school hours</li>
              <li>Fewer distractions: learning on paper with carefully chosen textbooks</li>
              <li>Lunches provided by the school each day. Set lunches, chef-curated daily</li>
              <li>Feedback every single class</li>
              <li>No more high-stakes assessments (except essential IB exams)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="exercise-q">It's the same change. How does each version make you feel? Which reasons for resistance does Version A trigger? Which strategies does Version B use? What would it still need?</div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Check</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">Version A triggers fear and uncertainty. Version B uses communication (framing, reasons). A full rollout would also explain why (evidence, what staff have seen) and ask for input on the details, such as how lunch service should run: that's participation.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 6: You're the HR director</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Think of a club you're actually in. As its HR director, which area (planning, recruitment, training, appraisal, dismissal) needs to change? Name the two most important internal and external factors behind that. What resistance will you meet, from which groups, and why? Describe how you'd roll out the change.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /practice -->

  <div class="page-nav">
    <a href="/ibbm/unit2/" class="prev">
      <span class="nav-label">← Previous</span>
      <span class="nav-title">Unit 2</span>
    </a>
    <a href="/ibbm/unit2/2-2/" class="next">
      <span class="nav-label">Next →</span>
      <span class="nav-title">2.2 Organizational Structure</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</Layout>
